    "passage": [
      "All living things are made of cells, the smallest units that carry out the processes of life. Cell theory holds that every organism is made of one or more cells, that the cell is the basic unit of structure and function, and that new cells arise only from existing cells by division.",
      "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria and archaea, have no nucleus: their DNA lies in a region of the cytoplasm called the nucleoid, and they lack membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotic cells, found in plants, animals, fungi and protists, keep their DNA inside a nucleus and contain membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotic cells are usually much larger.",
      "Organelles divide the work of a eukaryotic cell. Ribosomes build proteins; the endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus modify, package and ship them. Mitochondria release usable energy from food as ATP through cellular respiration. Plant cells also have chloroplasts, where photosynthesis happens, a cell wall of cellulose, and usually a large central vacuole.",
      "The cell membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins. It is selectively permeable: small nonpolar molecules such as oxygen cross it easily, while ions and large polar molecules need channel or carrier proteins. Diffusion and osmosis move substances down their concentration gradients without energy; active transport moves them against a gradient and uses ATP."
