    "topic": "Heredity",
    "passage": [
      "Gregor Mendel studied pea plants in the 1860s and found that traits are passed on as discrete units, now called genes. Each organism carries two copies, or alleles, of each gene, one from each parent. Different alleles produce different versions of a trait, such as purple or white flowers.",
      "When the two alleles differ, one may be dominant and mask the other, which is recessive. An organism's genotype is its pair of alleles, for example PP, Pp or pp; its phenotype is the trait it shows. PP and Pp plants both have purple flowers, so the same phenotype can come from different genotypes.",
      "Mendel's law of segregation states that the two alleles separate when gametes form, so each gamete carries only one. His law of independent assortment states that alleles of different genes are sorted into gametes independently, which holds for genes on different chromosomes.",
      "A Punnett square predicts offspring ratios. Crossing two Pp plants gives genotypes PP, Pp and pp in a 1:2:1 ratio and phenotypes in a 3:1 ratio of purple to white. These are probabilities: a real family of four offspring need not show exactly three purple and one white."
    ],
