      "Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace proposed natural selection as the main mechanism of evolution, published in 1858; Darwin's On the Origin of Species followed in 1859. Evolution is a change in the inherited characteristics of a population over generations.",
      "Natural selection rests on observable facts. Individuals in a population vary; much of that variation is inherited; more offspring are produced than can survive; and individuals with traits better suited to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more. Over generations those traits become more common.",
      "Variation arises from mutation and from the shuffling of alleles in sexual reproduction. Selection does not create variation on demand: an individual cannot develop a useful trait because it needs it, and traits acquired during a lifetime, such as stronger muscles, are not inherited.",
      "Evidence for evolution includes the fossil record, homologous structures such as the shared bone pattern of the human arm and the whale flipper, DNA similarities between species, and evolution observed directly, for example antibiotic resistance spreading in bacteria."
